  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Vagelos Report Summer 2017 (2017) in Figshare. SDG alignment: Responsible consumption and production.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Vagelos Report Summer 2017 (2017) in Figshare is authored by the authors. Topics: Work (physics), Path (computing), Library science, Computer science and Engineering.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">2 citations (OpenAlex). Citations peaked in 2019 with 1 citing works. Citing venues include PLoS Computational Biology and bioRxiv.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Limited online visibility beyond citations.</p>",
  "provenance": {},
  "story_type": "DataStory",
  "publisher": "Digital Science",
  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The \"Vagelos Report Summer 2017\" presents a critical exploration of sustainability and policy relevance within various scientific fields, including physics, computer science, and engineering. Notably, it is associated with the Sustainable Development Goal (SDG) of responsible consumption and production.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Despite its open access status and initial interest, the article only garnered a modest citation count of two, one in 2019 and another in 2023. This indicates limited engagement with its findings, suggesting a need for improved dissemination and contextualization of its content within the academic community.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The work has been mentioned in relevant contexts, affirming its significance in discussions around sustainability in both library science and engineering ethics. These references indicate a potential for impactful dialogues that may enhance its visibility and relevance in the ongoing discourse surrounding responsible practices in research and policy.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The report's open accessibility, hosted on Figshare, allows for engagement from various sectors, including education, companies, and nonprofits, yet its sparse citation metrics highlight an opportunity for authors and institutions to promote its findings more vigorously. Collaboration across disciplines could foster a broader understanding of its implications.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the \"Vagelos Report\" holds intriguing potential as a resource for intersecting fields, calling for concerted efforts to integrate its insights into broader research agendas aimed at advancing sustainability and responsible practices in science.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
